  "body": "Browsers automatically close the current `<p>`-tag if non-phrasing content, f.ex. a `<div>` is found before the closing `</p>` while parsing due to the tag omission rules for paragraphs. It should be possible to toggle this behavior on/off.\r\n\r\nThis would be convenient if, for example, one wants to build an inline-block element where it makes sense to use `<div>` etc. for its internal composition if it follows a typical block layout internally, while still being usable in a phrasing context.\r\n\r\n**The behavior we should be able to toggle on/off**\r\nThe code:\r\n```html\r\n<p>\r\n<div>...</div>\r\n</p>\r\n```\r\nResult when inspecting in a browser:\r\n```html\r\n<p> </p>\r\n<div>...</div>\r\n<p> </p>\r\n```\r\n",
